h daily events and major successes and failures.","confidence":0.72},"academicOrientation":{"score":45,"confidence":0.55},"profileConfidence":0.74,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralFigures":[{"name":"Roger Maris","confidence":0.42},{"name":"Babe Ruth","confidence":0.36}]},"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"3306d79bb9596076d44193c3050d6afa9d003eb772454450c7eee3f04c07e0ef","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Science","secondarySubjects":["Society & Culture"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily explores the scientific principles of randomness and probability, while also discussing their implications for social behavior and decision-making.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"5f53899da3a682b29466ba899591789259dae1c079637599799ac545b690ff30","summary":"In this irreverent and illuminating book, acclaimed writer and scientist Leonard Mlodinow shows us how randomness, change, and probability reveal a tremendous amount about our daily lives, and how we misunderstand the significance of everything from a casual conversation to a major financial setback. As a result, successes and failures in life are often attributed to clear and obvious cases, when in actuality they are more profoundly influenced by chance.The rise and fall of your favorite movie star of the most reviled CEO--in fact, of all our destinies--reflects as much as planning and innate abilities. Even the legendary Roger Maris, who beat Babe Ruth's single-season home run record, was in all likelihood not great but just lucky. And it might be shocking to realize that you are twice as likely to be killed in a car accident on your way to buying a lottery ticket than you are to win the lottery.How could it have happened that a wine was given five out of five stars, the highest rating, in one journal and in another it was called the worst wine of the decade? Mlodinow vividly demonstrates how wine